WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.417 --> 00:00:06.878
At the very young age
of less than one year old,

00:00:06.878 --> 00:00:09.673
a violent civil war broke out
in my home country

00:00:09.673 --> 00:00:12.008
of the Democratic Republic of Congo.

00:00:12.509 --> 00:00:15.011
As a result, my family was forced to flee.

00:00:15.011 --> 00:00:16.721
And as luck would have it,

00:00:16.721 --> 00:00:18.974
we found safety in Kyangwali refugee camp,

00:00:19.015 --> 00:00:20.684
located in western Uganda,

00:00:20.725 --> 00:00:23.144
where I grew up from
and have learned to call home

00:00:23.186 --> 00:00:25.063
for the past 24 years.

00:00:25.105 --> 00:00:28.191
Every morning, my two parents
would wake up at 5am

00:00:28.191 --> 00:00:31.653
and leave for the fields where they went
to cultivate our small garden,

00:00:31.695 --> 00:00:34.155
to plant maize and beans
to feed the family.

00:00:34.614 --> 00:00:36.658
And every evening
when they came back home,

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:38.743
they would end the night in the same way.

00:00:39.077 --> 00:00:41.204
Usually, they would gather me
and my siblings

00:00:41.204 --> 00:00:42.581
and would give us a pep talk

00:00:42.581 --> 00:00:46.585
on how much they desired for us
to go to school and be educated.

00:00:46.585 --> 00:00:49.462
Surprisingly, none of my parents
actually had gone to school.

00:00:49.504 --> 00:00:52.549
But to them, education
meant securing a well-paying job

00:00:52.591 --> 00:00:54.134
at some point in the future.

00:00:54.175 --> 00:00:57.262
And with this, we [could] afford
the three meals a day.

00:00:57.304 --> 00:01:00.432
We perhaps could build
a nice-looking house for our family

00:01:00.432 --> 00:01:04.519
or even decide to take our family
from the refugee camp to the city.

00:01:04.853 --> 00:01:07.439
And so when I started going to school,

00:01:07.439 --> 00:01:11.276
I took this [to] heart
and I took school seriously.

00:01:11.276 --> 00:01:15.280
Even though I was just a little kid,
I managed to wake myself out of bed,

00:01:15.322 --> 00:01:16.906
take all three goats to graze

00:01:16.948 --> 00:01:19.993
and be back on time
to dress up and go to school.

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:23.413
Classes in the refugee camp
can be overcrowded.

00:01:23.455 --> 00:01:27.167
I was one of the nearly 200 students
who sat in a single classroom

00:01:27.167 --> 00:01:29.002
as I tried to focus and learn.

00:01:29.419 --> 00:01:32.047
But besides the problem
of overcrowded classrooms,

00:01:32.088 --> 00:01:34.799
we also had an issue
whereby most of our learning

00:01:34.841 --> 00:01:37.594
was characterized by rote memorization.

00:01:37.927 --> 00:01:40.597
On a typical day,
we showed up in a classroom,

00:01:40.639 --> 00:01:42.557
copied down notes that teachers gave us

00:01:42.599 --> 00:01:44.643
and were expected to go back home

00:01:44.684 --> 00:01:46.853
[and] cram these in preparation for exams.

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:50.315
And if I can be honest with you,
I did not enjoy this.

00:01:50.815 --> 00:01:55.028
We barely interacted with our teachers
and even amongst ourselves.

00:01:55.403 --> 00:01:57.447
And even though as serious students,

00:01:57.489 --> 00:02:00.116
most of the time,
school seemed to be very boring.

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:02.160
However, as I was growing up

00:02:02.202 --> 00:02:05.121
and as I took on the routine
of going to school in the morning

00:02:05.121 --> 00:02:07.457
and then coming back
to my family in the evening,

00:02:07.457 --> 00:02:09.751
there seemed to be one thing
and one thing only,

00:02:09.751 --> 00:02:11.503
that truly held my attention,

00:02:11.544 --> 00:02:13.421
and that was the idea of playing games.

00:02:13.755 --> 00:02:16.383
And I most loved playing
the game of football.

00:02:16.424 --> 00:02:19.969
Now I will start off by addressing
the biggest elephant in the room.

00:02:20.011 --> 00:02:22.305
I know most of you love American football,

00:02:22.347 --> 00:02:25.183
but actually that's not the game
I'm talking about.

00:02:25.809 --> 00:02:28.311
I’m five foot three, and given my size,

00:02:28.353 --> 00:02:31.106
I truly can't play American football.

00:02:31.106 --> 00:02:35.068
So I’m actually talking about football,
where we use our feet, and that’s soccer.

00:02:35.443 --> 00:02:38.071
I also loved playing the cards of spades.

00:02:38.071 --> 00:02:40.865
And most of the time after school
I would come to my house,

00:02:40.865 --> 00:02:42.826
and I would be holding a deck of cards.

00:02:42.826 --> 00:02:45.995
And every time my parents saw me
shuffling these cards,

00:02:46.037 --> 00:02:48.957
they would be paranoid,
thinking I'd started gambling.

00:02:48.957 --> 00:02:50.166
But I wasn't.

00:02:50.166 --> 00:02:52.877
And perhaps because I then
didn't have money to do so.

00:02:53.503 --> 00:02:54.671
No wonder,

00:02:54.671 --> 00:02:56.756
while in high school in 2016,

00:02:56.756 --> 00:03:00.510
the idea of gamification
became the best solution

00:03:00.552 --> 00:03:01.886
I thought about I could use

00:03:01.928 --> 00:03:04.472
to be able to address the problem
in the refugee camp.

00:03:04.514 --> 00:03:05.682
And since then,

00:03:05.682 --> 00:03:08.685
I've been on a quest
to make education more accessible

00:03:08.685 --> 00:03:11.980
and more fun and engaging for children
within Kyangwali refugee camp

00:03:12.021 --> 00:03:13.606
through the use of board games.

00:03:13.606 --> 00:03:14.899
And if you don't mind,

00:03:14.941 --> 00:03:17.736
I'm going to share
with you how it all happened.

00:03:18.361 --> 00:03:20.780
One year before, in 2015,

00:03:20.822 --> 00:03:23.366
I was lucky and got an opportunity to go

00:03:23.366 --> 00:03:25.785
and study at African Leadership Academy,

00:03:25.785 --> 00:03:27.996
located in Johannesburg, South Africa.

00:03:28.037 --> 00:03:33.126
And here I was introduced to the idea
of entrepreneurship for the first time.

00:03:33.168 --> 00:03:35.003
And my amazing teachers taught me

00:03:35.044 --> 00:03:37.922
that entrepreneurs
were simply problem solvers.

00:03:37.964 --> 00:03:42.010
And I immediately knew that I wanted
to work on an education problem.

00:03:42.677 --> 00:03:46.139
The first idea I thought:
I could build a primary school.

00:03:46.181 --> 00:03:48.767
More children would
access learning spaces,

00:03:48.808 --> 00:03:52.520
teachers would not have the problem
of overcrowded classrooms.

00:03:52.562 --> 00:03:56.941
But the problem was I was 19 years old,
didn’t have the money,

00:03:56.983 --> 00:04:00.153
didn't have the skills of what it took
to build a primary school.

00:04:00.195 --> 00:04:01.696
The second idea I thought:

00:04:01.738 --> 00:04:04.783
I could advocate
for a well-equipped library.

00:04:04.824 --> 00:04:07.911
With this, we would have enough
textbooks in the library,

00:04:07.952 --> 00:04:10.955
and children would have enough
reading materials.

00:04:10.955 --> 00:04:14.167
They would do it at school
in performing in the national exams.

00:04:14.209 --> 00:04:17.003
However, I had one issue with the library.

00:04:17.337 --> 00:04:20.131
You see, food can be scarce
in the refugee camp,

00:04:20.173 --> 00:04:22.509
and several times
when I showed up at school,

00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:25.637
I personally remembered every time
I entered in the library,

00:04:25.678 --> 00:04:27.514
all I could think about was food.

00:04:28.431 --> 00:04:31.810
More than anything, I needed
a solution that was cheap,

00:04:31.851 --> 00:04:33.269
a solution that was credible,

00:04:33.269 --> 00:04:36.648
and something that would be
very exciting to the children.

00:04:36.689 --> 00:04:38.942
Something that somehow
would help these children

00:04:38.942 --> 00:04:41.736
forget about their hunger
and then engage with running.

00:04:41.778 --> 00:04:44.531
But also something that would help
teachers have control

00:04:44.572 --> 00:04:46.449
on overcrowded classrooms.

00:04:46.491 --> 00:04:47.909
But most importantly,

00:04:47.951 --> 00:04:51.955
help children engage with algebra,
with the science they were learning,

00:04:51.996 --> 00:04:55.083
and have fun every single day
they came to school.

00:04:55.583 --> 00:04:58.545
And so even though I had never
taken drawing classes,

00:04:58.545 --> 00:05:02.090
I picked up a pencil and got some courage
to start making sketches

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:03.967
of what would be my first board game.

00:05:04.342 --> 00:05:06.928
I also generated
a number of trivia questions

00:05:06.970 --> 00:05:10.640
and then called up my former primary
school teacher in Kyangwali refugee camp.

00:05:10.682 --> 00:05:14.060
And I asked if I could visit
my primary school once again.

00:05:14.102 --> 00:05:15.687
He was generous, and he said yes.

00:05:16.229 --> 00:05:18.523
When I showed up at my former
primary school,

00:05:18.565 --> 00:05:20.358
the kids were excited,

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:22.402
and most of them
jumped out of their seats

00:05:22.443 --> 00:05:25.405
when I told them what I held
in my hands was a board game.

00:05:25.738 --> 00:05:29.033
Surprisingly, none of them actually
knew even how the game was played.

00:05:29.075 --> 00:05:31.786
But heading towards the board game,
they were thrilled.

00:05:32.495 --> 00:05:35.164
Working with the teachers
and the school administration,

00:05:35.206 --> 00:05:38.835
they give me about one hour to test out
the game for the next couple weeks.

00:05:39.252 --> 00:05:41.421
And the game was a huge success.

00:05:41.462 --> 00:05:45.258
So much so, that the next morning
my mom woke me out of bed

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:47.427
telling me that there was
a group of children

00:05:47.468 --> 00:05:50.179
wearing my school uniform, looking for me.

00:05:50.763 --> 00:05:54.142
I was still confused because it was
about 6am in the morning.

00:05:54.475 --> 00:05:57.395
But I woke up out of my bed,
still in my night dress actually,

00:05:57.437 --> 00:05:58.980
and then opened the door.

00:05:59.022 --> 00:06:00.565
There was about six children,

00:06:00.607 --> 00:06:02.317
and when I looked at them,

00:06:02.358 --> 00:06:04.777
there were about two faces
that I could recognize.

00:06:04.819 --> 00:06:06.946
There was James and Patrick,

00:06:06.988 --> 00:06:10.116
two of the children
I had worked with the previous day.

00:06:10.742 --> 00:06:13.786
And with the most innocent faces
that I have ever seen,

00:06:13.828 --> 00:06:16.664
they were like, “Could you come
and join us at school,

00:06:16.706 --> 00:06:19.167
and then we play the game
before school starts?”

00:06:19.667 --> 00:06:22.837
And looking at their faces,
there was no way I was going to say no.

00:06:22.837 --> 00:06:25.465
I said, "I'll dress up
and meet you over by school."

00:06:26.007 --> 00:06:30.303
And this group of amazing children
became my early testers.

00:06:30.345 --> 00:06:32.180
We would play the game in the morning,

00:06:32.180 --> 00:06:35.850
and then in the afternoon, as they waited
for their afternoon shift.

00:06:36.267 --> 00:06:37.435
And today,

00:06:37.435 --> 00:06:40.605
the game has evolved into what
my team and I at My Home Stars,

00:06:40.605 --> 00:06:42.565
a nonprofit we are running in Uganda,

00:06:42.565 --> 00:06:45.985
have called the 5 STA-Z
educational board game.

00:06:46.027 --> 00:06:48.863
And here is how the 5 STA-Z
education board works.

00:06:49.364 --> 00:06:50.573
At the beginning,

00:06:50.615 --> 00:06:54.077
a classroom divides itself
into groups of five children.

00:06:54.577 --> 00:06:58.081
And these children
begin interacting with one another

00:06:58.122 --> 00:07:00.166
on questions and content we've developed

00:07:00.208 --> 00:07:03.002
in partnership
with a group of 16 teachers.

00:07:03.461 --> 00:07:06.339
All this content is a reflection
of the actual content

00:07:06.381 --> 00:07:09.926
covered in the Ugandan educational
curriculum of mathematics,

00:07:09.968 --> 00:07:12.887
English, science and social studies.

00:07:13.346 --> 00:07:14.931
And as they interact,

00:07:14.973 --> 00:07:18.977
they get to earn points
for every trivia question they get right.

00:07:18.977 --> 00:07:23.064
And they use these points to advance
around the star-shaped board

00:07:23.064 --> 00:07:25.608
with a goal of being the first one
to enter the middle

00:07:25.650 --> 00:07:27.860
and take on the big yellow star,

00:07:27.902 --> 00:07:30.113
which usually gets children very excited.

00:07:30.905 --> 00:07:34.158
My team and I also have worked together
to incorporate game mechanics

00:07:34.158 --> 00:07:35.535
such as chance and jeopardy,

00:07:35.535 --> 00:07:37.996
where children actually
can earn extra points

00:07:37.996 --> 00:07:42.000
when they collaborate and help each other
with difficult trivia questions.

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:43.710
This has been intended to make sure

00:07:43.710 --> 00:07:46.879
that children actually are learning
the components of communication

00:07:46.879 --> 00:07:48.339
and working together in teams.

00:07:48.381 --> 00:07:50.925
But also it makes learning
become more child-centered.

00:07:50.967 --> 00:07:53.845
And actually, teachers can focus
on groups of children

00:07:53.886 --> 00:07:55.513
that are struggling most,

00:07:55.555 --> 00:07:57.724
a model that we are finding
to be very helpful

00:07:57.724 --> 00:07:59.517
in communities such as refugee camps,

00:07:59.559 --> 00:08:02.562
where most of the time
we don't have enough teachers.

00:08:02.603 --> 00:08:04.188
And there's one particular reason

00:08:04.188 --> 00:08:07.316
as to why my team and I decided
to call the game the 5 STA-Z.

00:08:07.316 --> 00:08:10.403
And it's simply the conviction
that no matter where you're from

00:08:10.445 --> 00:08:12.864
and the current existing circumstances,

00:08:12.864 --> 00:08:14.949
we can all reach for the stars.

00:08:15.700 --> 00:08:18.119
And like Neil deGrasse Tyson would put it,

00:08:18.119 --> 00:08:20.204
not only do we live among the stars,

00:08:20.204 --> 00:08:22.498
the stars themselves live within us.

00:08:22.540 --> 00:08:25.168
And that includes children
from refugee camps.

00:08:25.710 --> 00:08:27.837
And as children also start
playing the game,

00:08:27.879 --> 00:08:30.131
we encourage them
not to use their actual names,

00:08:30.173 --> 00:08:35.094
but actually to take on the characters
of the five brightest stars in the galaxy.

00:08:35.136 --> 00:08:40.349
And that’s Canopus, Rigel,
Arcturus, Vega and Sirius.

00:08:40.725 --> 00:08:42.769
And also, when we hand them
the board game,

00:08:42.810 --> 00:08:44.812
we remind them that they are already stars

00:08:44.812 --> 00:08:46.564
who are interacting with one another

00:08:46.606 --> 00:08:49.233
to help each other
just shine a little bit brighter.

00:08:49.567 --> 00:08:53.321
And perhaps our biggest impact
was realized in 2020 and 2021.

00:08:53.863 --> 00:08:55.364
Like most of you remember,

00:08:55.364 --> 00:08:58.701
these are the two years when COVID-19
pandemic decided to visit us.

00:08:59.243 --> 00:09:03.039
And during this time,
classes in the refugee camp closed,

00:09:03.081 --> 00:09:06.793
and children were sent home with no way
of accessing learning resources.

00:09:07.418 --> 00:09:10.880
My team and I took on the challenge
to make learning happen within homes,

00:09:10.922 --> 00:09:13.466
and we produced about 1,000 game packages

00:09:13.508 --> 00:09:15.718
that we gave to teachers
and community leaders.

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:19.472
And with this, we’re able to help
about 5,000 children

00:09:19.514 --> 00:09:22.558
to access home learning
in the most fun and engaging way

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:24.227
through the use of board games.

00:09:24.227 --> 00:09:27.563
Today, we are working with five schools
in Kyangwali refugee camp

00:09:27.605 --> 00:09:30.691
where we’ve actually been able to work
with school administration

00:09:30.691 --> 00:09:33.319
to make the 5 STA-Z part
of the learning schedules.

00:09:33.361 --> 00:09:34.529
In the morning,

00:09:34.570 --> 00:09:38.157
children spend the morning time
learning the normal classes

00:09:38.199 --> 00:09:40.243
they're supposed to take in the curriculum

00:09:40.243 --> 00:09:42.620
and then spend about one hour
in the afternoon

00:09:42.662 --> 00:09:45.581
to put what they've learned
in the morning into practice.

00:09:46.207 --> 00:09:49.877
Beyond being a game that is helping
children master the curriculum content

00:09:49.919 --> 00:09:52.130
and do well in the national exam,

00:09:52.171 --> 00:09:55.133
my team and I are committed
to continue building the 5 STA-Z

00:09:55.174 --> 00:09:56.342
into a learning resource

00:09:56.384 --> 00:09:59.470
that will continue to put a smile
on these children's faces,

00:09:59.512 --> 00:10:01.597
every single day they come to class

00:10:01.597 --> 00:10:03.975
and a game that can continue
to inspire them

00:10:04.016 --> 00:10:07.478
into what is possible
for them in the future.

00:10:07.478 --> 00:10:10.189
And also, we are committed
to continue building this game

00:10:10.189 --> 00:10:13.484
into a game that we shall be able to make
available to other children

00:10:13.526 --> 00:10:15.361
in other refugee camps across Uganda

00:10:15.403 --> 00:10:18.114
and other countries
across the African continent.

00:10:18.614 --> 00:10:19.782
In the past one year,

00:10:19.824 --> 00:10:23.494
about 100 million people across
the world have been displaced,

00:10:23.536 --> 00:10:26.289
and many of these
are the result of a civil war.

00:10:26.914 --> 00:10:30.084
From Kyangwali refugee camp
in western Uganda

00:10:30.084 --> 00:10:31.252
to New York City

00:10:31.294 --> 00:10:34.547
and standing in front of you today
and engaging with you

00:10:34.589 --> 00:10:37.717
is a big, big, big privilege.

00:10:38.217 --> 00:10:41.512
And the only reason I'm able to do so
and stand in front of you,

00:10:41.512 --> 00:10:45.099
is because I've been given
an opportunity to go to school.

00:10:45.099 --> 00:10:47.185
And while board games such as the 5 STA-Z

00:10:47.185 --> 00:10:50.104
will not be able to solve some
of the world's complex problems

00:10:50.146 --> 00:10:51.856
of the 21st century,

00:10:51.898 --> 00:10:54.317
like refugee crisis and displacement,

00:10:54.358 --> 00:10:57.486
I strongly believe that you and I
that are here today,

00:10:57.528 --> 00:10:59.614
that happen to be
a little bit privileged,

00:10:59.614 --> 00:11:03.284
bear the responsibility to continue
building platforms and systems

00:11:03.326 --> 00:11:08.164
that will help refugee children
imagine a better future for themselves,

00:11:08.206 --> 00:11:10.625
their families and their communities.

00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:12.168
Thank you.

00:11:12.210 --> 00:11:15.379
(Applause)

